Z||000000||Alex4mula||05-30-2001||10:49 AM||||Can't believe I learned this 13 years after I bought my car. This weekend went on a long trip to visit relatives. Returning my car averaged about 25 mpg (200 miles on 8 gal). Went to gas station and pistol clicked on 8 gallons as always  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">. Started pushing in gas with a click about every 0.15-0.25 gallons. I was in disbelieve when I was able to put 10.9 gallons! That's near 3 gallons more of my normal fillup and one shy of the 11.9 tank. Which is ok because I may have about that still in. That means that in another long trip I may get 250 miles, or in city driving I may get to the 200 when I usually get 150  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. Just need to be patient in the gas station and keep clicking...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000001||SteveJ||05-30-2001||11:12 AM||||Overfill too much and you will push gas into the vapor vent tube and on into the vapor canister which will destroy it. See the Ogre's cave.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Alex4mula||05-30-2001||12:16 PM||||How do you define overfill? Trying to put more than 11.9 gals? Getting near 11.9? 11?||||reg||1
Z||000003||SteveJ||05-30-2001||12:27 PM||||Quote from the Ogre's cave, "There are only a couple things that can go wrong with the canister. It can be damaged by cracking the thing or by over filling the gas tank. “Topping off” the gas tank can force fuel up the vent line into the can and ruin it. While the fuel pump nozzle is supposed to shut off when the tank is full, they are often bad and can pressurize the fuel tank enough to force fuel up the vent. Certain types of “Vapor Recovery” nozzles are great for this."<P>Find it under engine, emissions, Fuel Vapor Canister.<P>||||reg||1
